image.asymptote:
import three;
size(9cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(8,-10,7);
triple O0=(0,0,0), A=(3,0,0), B=(3,2,0), C=(0,2,0);
triple D=(0,0,2), E=(3,0,2), F=(3,2,2), G=(0,2,2);
pen face=rgb(0.55,0.67,0.84)+opacity(0.13);
pen edge=gray(0.38)+linewidth(0.65pt);
draw(surface(O0--A--B--C--cycle),face);
draw(surface(D--E--F--G--cycle),face);
draw(surface(O0--A--E--D--cycle),face);
draw(surface(A--B--F--E--cycle),face);
draw(surface(B--C--G--F--cycle),face);
draw(surface(C--O0--D--G--cycle),face);
draw(O0--A--B--C--cycle,edge);
draw(D--E--F--G--cycle,edge);
draw(O0--D,edge); draw(A--E,edge);
draw(B--F,edge); draw(C--G,edge);
pen entering=rgb(0.13,0.37,0.74)+linewidth(1.5pt);
pen leaving=rgb(0.78,0.18,0.14)+linewidth(1.5pt);
draw((-1.3,0.6,1)--(0,0.6,1),entering,Arrow3);
draw((-1.3,1.4,1)--(0,1.4,1),entering,Arrow3);
draw((3,0.6,1)--(4.3,0.6,1),leaving,Arrow3);
draw((3,1.4,1)--(4.3,1.4,1),leaving,Arrow3);
label("inward $\mathbf J$",(-1.3,1,1),2W,entering);
label("outward $\mathbf J$",(4.3,1,1),2E,leaving);
label("$E,\ \rho(t)$",(1.5,1,1),2N);

subsection.p:
For a fixed solid region E, the stored amount is
\boxed{\iiint_E \rho\,dV.}
The outward flux through the boundary is
\boxed{\iint_{\partial E}\mathbf J\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dS.}

The sign convention is important:
\begin{array}{c|c}
  \mathbf J\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\gt 0 \amp \text{net flow outward}\\[1mm]
  \mathbf J\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\lt 0 \amp \text{net flow inward}\\[1mm]
  \mathbf J\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}=0 \amp \text{no crossing through that surface}
\end{array}

Some quantities are not conserved by transport alone. Ink may be injected into water. Heat
may be produced by a heater. A chemical reaction may produce a tracked chemical species;
total electric charge, unlike an individual species, remains conserved.

In form language, the same law is
\boxed{\frac{d}{dt}\int_E \rho\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz
  +
  \int_{\partial E}\Phi_{\mathbf J}
  =
  \int_E s\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz.}
